But turning that into a general theoretical law... is premature. Because some epistemic systems legitimately perform retrospective revision... So the safer proposition is: $$
\boxed{
\text{History access by the kernel is an explicit architectural capability requiring adjudication.}
}
$$

The problem is: $$
K = Zero(...)
$$ when `Zero` is declared a lens and \(K\) a domain object. So the better rule is semantic: $$
\boxed{
\text{A lens cannot be used as a domain object or as the ontological producer of the object it examines.}
}
$$

If a claim does not specify an ordering, it may be: underspecified, non-operational, not evaluable under the current progress predicate, but that does not necessarily mean: unfalsifiable in the Popperian sense. I'd use: $$
\boxed{
\text{not currently testable as progress claims}
}
$$

